"VMMaker.oscog-cb.3825: compile abstract instructions into the future survivor space instead of alloca'ing them on the C stack.  The future survivor space is empty except during a scavenge, and compiling a method never scavenges, so it can serve as scratch memory for the abstract opcodes and fixups.  Methods whose opcodes and fixups would not fit fall back to the interpreter (MethodTooBig). Adds SpurMemoryManager API to access the future survivor space from the Cogit."!!SpurMemoryManager methodsFor: 'cog jit support' stamp: 'cb 10/26/2026 23:00'!futureSurvivorSpaceStart	"Answer the start of the future survivor space.  It is empty except during a scavenge, so the	 Cogit uses it as scratch memory while compiling; see Cogit>>allocateOpcodes:bytecodes:ifFail:."	<api>	<returnTypeC: #usqInt>	^scavenger futureSpace start! !!SpurMemoryManager methodsFor: 'cog jit support' stamp: 'cb 10/26/2026 23:00'!futureSurvivorSpaceBytes	"Answer the size of the future survivor space; see futureSurvivorSpaceStart."	<api>	^scavenger futureSpace limit - scavenger futureSpace start! !!SpurMemoryManager methodsFor: 'cog jit support' stamp: 'cb 10/26/2026 23:00'!futureSurvivorSpaceIsEmpty	"Answer if nothing has been copied into the future survivor space, i.e. if no scavenge is in progress."	<api>	^scavenger futureSurvivorStart = scavenger futureSpace start! !!Cogit methodsFor: 'initialization' stamp: 'cb 10/26/2026 23:00'!allocateOpcodes: numberOfAbstractOpcodes bytecodes: numberOfBytecodes ifFail: failBlock	"Allocate the various arrays needed to compile abstract instructions, failing if the size	 needed is considered too high.  Notionally we only need as many fixups as there are	 bytecodes.  But we reuse fixups to record pc-dependent instructions in	 generateInstructionsAt: and so need at least as many as there are abstract opcodes.	 In C the arrays are allocated in the future survivor space, which is empty except during a	 scavenge (and compiling never scavenges), rather than alloca'ed on the C stack.  Fail if they	 do not fit.  The opcodes and fixups are allocated as one block, the fixups following the opcodes."	<inline: true>	| opcodeBytes fixupBytes allocBytes |	numAbstractOpcodes := numberOfAbstractOpcodes.	opcodeBytes := (self sizeof: CogAbstractInstruction) * numAbstractOpcodes.	fixupBytes := (self sizeof: CogBytecodeFixup) * numAbstractOpcodes.	allocBytes := opcodeBytes + fixupBytes.	"Document the fact that the MaxStackAllocSize ensures that the number of abstract	 opcodes fits in a 16 bit integer (e.g. CogBytecodeFixup's instructionIndex)."	self assert: (self sizeof: CogAbstractInstruction) + (self sizeof: CogBytecodeFixup) * 49152 > MaxStackAllocSize.	(allocBytes > MaxStackAllocSize	 or: [allocBytes > (self cCode: [objectMemory futureSurvivorSpaceBytes] inSmalltalk: [MaxStackAllocSize])]) ifTrue:		[^failBlock value].	self		cCode:			[self assert: objectMemory futureSurvivorSpaceIsEmpty.			 abstractOpcodes := self cCoerceSimple: objectMemory futureSurvivorSpaceStart to: #'AbstractInstruction *'.			 self b: abstractOpcodes zero: allocBytes.			 fixups := (abstractOpcodes asUnsignedInteger + opcodeBytes) asVoidPointer]		inSmalltalk:			[abstractOpcodes := CArrayAccessor on:									 ((1 to: numAbstractOpcodes) collect: [:ign| CogCompilerClass for: self]).			 fixups := CArrayAccessor on:						((1 to: numAbstractOpcodes) collect: [:ign| self bytecodeFixupClass for: self])].	self zeroOpcodeIndexForNewOpcodes.	labelCounter := 0! !